# Printing ggproto objects: print.ggproto

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`print(<ggproto>)`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/print.ggproto.html), which covers
`format(<ggproto>)` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code
first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what
it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

Printing a ggproto object calls the object's own `print` method if it has one. Otherwise it lists
the object's members, its own and those it inherits: in one flat list by default, or, with
`flat = FALSE`, level by level up the inheritance. `format()` returns that listing as text. Not
in Giggly yet: `ggproto()`, so there are no ggproto objects to print.

## An object with a print method

```r
Dog <- ggproto(
  print = function(self, n) {
    cat("Woof!\n")
  }
 )
Dog
```

Printing `Dog` calls its own `print` method, which writes `Woof!`. Not in Giggly yet:
`ggproto()`.

## Formatting an object as text

```r
cat(format(Dog), "\n")
```

`format()` does not call the object's `print` method: it describes the object, listing its
members, and `cat()` writes that text. Not in Giggly yet: `ggproto()`.
